  "id": "Compound::DB00883 - Gene::2977",
  "verbalization": "Isosorbide Dinitrate is implicated in the regulation of vascular tone through its interaction with nitrate esters, which are a class of vasodilating agents. This relationship is supported by multiple paths showing that Isosorbide Dinitrate causes or is associated with conditions like rebound hypertension or withdrawal hypertension, both of which are linked to Nitroglycerin. Nitroglycerin, in turn, binds to Gucy1a2, a G-protein coupled receptor involved in vascular smooth muscle relaxation. The shared lowest common ancestors—Nitrate Ester and Vasodilating Agent—suggest a common mechanism of action involving nitric oxide and vascular dilation. The evidence indicates that Isosorbide Dinitrate may exert its effects through a pathway that involves Nitroglycerin, which activates Gucy1a2, thereby contributing to the observed vascular effects. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Gucy1a2 in the vasodilatory response triggered by Isosorbide Dinitrate, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
